{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":2114492,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,9]],"date-time":"2026-04-09T17:56:59Z","timestamp":1775757419854,"version":"3.50.1"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":56,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Structural fracture events in civil infrastructure are rare yet catastrophic, creating a fundamental challenge for data-driven structural health monitoring: the absence of labeled failure data. This study proposes an interpretable zero-shot learning framework for detecting structural degradation precursors without relying on damage labels. The method integrates graph neural networks (GNNs), temporal contrastive learning, and Dirichlet stochastic weight averaging into a unified architecture termed DSWA-GNN. By modeling structural systems as temporally evolving graphs, the framework captures both spatial member interactions and progressive degradation dynamics. The approach is validated on the FracGraph-90 benchmark, a curated graph-based structural health dataset comprising three bridge systems with 30-step degradation histories. Results demonstrate strong alignment between zero-shot anomaly scores and cumulative damage progression, achieving a Spearman correlation of \u03c1 = 0.75 without exposure to any failure examples during training. Ablation studies confirm that relational modeling and temporal contrastive pretraining are critical for capturing degradation trajectories, while ensemble stabilization enhances embedding robustness and inter-bridge separability. Importantly, interpretability analysis using GNNExplainer reveals that anomaly predictions are driven by physically meaningful members and connectivity pathways consistent with known load redistribution mechanisms. This interpretability enables actionable insights for inspection prioritization and risk-informed infrastructure management. The proposed DSWA-GNN framework advances structural health monitoring by enabling scalable, label-free, and interpretable detection of emerging structural risks. The results demonstrate the feasibility of zero-shot learning for predictive infrastructure monitoring and establish a foundation for integrating graph-based machine learning with structural mechanics in safety-critical applications.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6550921","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,9]],"date-time":"2026-04-09T16:42:38Z","timestamp":1775752958000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Graph Ensemble Learning for Zero-Shot Prediction of Structural Fracture Precursors in Civil Infrastructure"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Vignesh","family":"Jagajeevan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Vidhyalakshmi","family":"Sivakumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","reference":[{"key":"ref1","first-page":"1","article-title":"A Comprehensive Review on Emerging Nanocomposite Materials in Cementitious Coatings for Sustainable Corrosion Protection of Steel Structures","author":"J Vignesh","year":"2025","journal-title":"Arabian Journal for Science and Engineering"},{"key":"ref2","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1","DOI":"10.1080\/01694243.2026.2614359","article-title":"Development of hybrid coating systems for concrete steel structures emphasizing fire protection and long-term durability","author":"J Vignesh","year":"2026","journal-title":"Journal of Adhesion Science and Technology"},{"key":"ref3","first-page":"207","author":"A Yosefi","year":"2025","journal-title":"Identification of Damages in Concrete and Steel Structures: A Comprehensive Review. Damage Detection and Structural Health Monitoring of Concrete and Masonry Structures: Novel Techniques and Ap